 id:"R65-01", title:"The Long Road of Paper", category:"History", band:6.5, mock:null, mins:20,
 paras:[
 ["A","Few objects are as ordinary today as a sheet of paper, yet the material took more than a thousand years to travel from its birthplace in China to the workshops of Western Europe. Its journey was not a simple handover from one civilisation to the next. At almost every stage, the people who adopted paper changed the raw materials, the tools and the purposes for which it was used, so that the paper which eventually reached Europe was in many respects a different product from the one first made in East Asia."],
 ["B","Chinese tradition credits a court official named Cai Lun with presenting a method of paper-making to the emperor in the year 105 CE. However, archaeologists have recovered fragments of paper from tombs and watchtowers that appear to be considerably older, suggesting that he improved an existing process rather than inventing it. Early Chinese paper was made from the inner bark of the mulberry tree, from hemp and even from worn-out fishing nets, which were beaten into a pulp, mixed with water and lifted out on a fine screen to dry. At first the material was used mainly for wrapping goods and padding fragile objects. Only gradually did it replace strips of bamboo and expensive silk as the preferred surface for writing."],
 ["C","By the early seventh century, the technique had reached Korea and Japan. Buddhist monks played an important part in this movement, since their monasteries required large numbers of copies of sacred texts. Japanese craftsmen, unable to obtain exactly the same plants as their Chinese teachers, experimented with the fibres of local shrubs and developed sheets that were remarkably thin yet strong. Paper of this kind is still valued by conservators, who use it to repair damaged books and prints all over the world."],
 ["D","The westward spread of paper is often linked to a single dramatic event. According to a popular story, Chinese soldiers captured after a battle near the Talas River in 751 taught the craft to their captors in the city of Samarkand. Many historians now treat this account with caution, regarding it as a convenient legend that simplifies a slower process; paper had probably been carried along the trade routes of Central Asia for some time before the battle. Whatever the precise route, Samarkand soon became famous for the quality of its paper."],
 ["E","From Central Asia the craft moved into the heart of the Islamic world, and a paper mill was operating in Baghdad by the end of the eighth century. Government administrators were among its most enthusiastic supporters. Parchment, made from animal skin, could be scraped clean and rewritten, whereas ink soaked into paper, so any attempt to alter a document left visible damage. Paper was also far cheaper to produce. Because mulberry trees were scarce, papermakers in the region turned instead to linen rags and hemp. Within two centuries, mills had appeared in Damascus, Cairo and across North Africa, and a lively trade in books developed in many cities."],
 ["F","Paper entered Europe through Muslim Spain, and one of the earliest European mills on record was working in the town of Xàtiva in the twelfth century. Christian Europe did not welcome the new material with open arms. Some authorities considered it too fragile for documents that needed to last, and at least one ruler forbade its use for official records, insisting that parchment remain the standard. Such restrictions slowed the adoption of paper but did not stop it."],
 ["G","The decisive changes took place in Italy, particularly in the town of Fabriano during the thirteenth century. There, rags were first left to soak and ferment before being pounded by water-powered hammers, which produced pulp far more quickly than human labour. The pulp was then lifted out on a wire screen, and the sheets were dipped in animal gelatine rather than coated with starch, so that ink did not spread and the paper resisted decay. Papermakers also twisted wire into simple designs on the screen, leaving a faint watermark that identified each mill. When the printing press appeared in the middle of the fifteenth century, Europe therefore already possessed a reliable supply of affordable paper. Without it, printing could hardly have flourished, since a single large book on parchment might require the skins of hundreds of animals."]
 ],
