 id:"R65-03", title:"Minds in Eight Arms", category:"Biology", band:6.5, mock:null, mins:20,
 paras:[
 ["A","Stories of octopuses escaping from aquarium tanks, raiding neighbouring enclosures at night and returning before morning have circulated for decades. Until fairly recently, most scientists treated such tales as entertaining anecdotes rather than serious evidence. Over the past thirty years, however, a growing body of research has led many biologists to accept that octopuses possess a form of intelligence that rivals that of some birds and mammals, even if there is still considerable disagreement about what that intelligence consists of."],
 ["B","Octopuses belong to a group of molluscs known as cephalopods, which also includes squid and cuttlefish. Their closest relatives are snails and clams, animals not generally associated with complex behaviour. Many researchers believe that the key to the octopus's mental abilities lies in its evolutionary history. Its distant ancestors lost the protective shell that most molluscs still carry, leaving them soft-bodied and vulnerable. To survive, they had to become skilled at hunting, hiding and escaping, and these demands may have favoured larger and more flexible nervous systems."],
 ["C","Laboratory experiments have provided much of the evidence. In a series of trials at the Marlow Bay Marine Station, the comparative psychologist Tomas Rehnberg presented common octopuses with crabs sealed inside glass jars. Within a few sessions most animals had learned to unscrew the lids, and several became noticeably faster with practice. Rehnberg was particularly struck by one individual which, after failing to open a jar by pulling at it, paused and then began twisting instead. He also recorded octopuses repeatedly pushing a floating bottle into the current of the water inlet and catching it as it returned, behaviour which he describes as a form of play because it brought the animal no food or other obvious reward."],
 ["D","The structure of the octopus nervous system is unusual. An octopus has roughly five hundred million neurons, a number comparable to that of a dog, but about two-thirds of them are located not in its brain but in its arms. According to the neurobiologist Adaeze Okonkwo, this arrangement means that each arm enjoys a surprising degree of independence. In her experiments, arms that had been separated from the central brain continued to reach towards food and grasp it. She suggests that the brain sets general goals while the arms work out many of the details themselves, a division of labour that has no close equivalent in vertebrates."],
 ["E","Not everyone is convinced that such findings demonstrate intelligence in any meaningful sense. The behavioural ecologist Marguerite Calloway argues that much of what is reported could result from trial and error combined with natural curiosity, rather than from planning or understanding. She also warns that laboratory tests are often designed around human ideas of cleverness, such as solving puzzles with tools, and may therefore reward animals whose abilities happen to resemble our own while overlooking others. In her view, researchers should study how octopuses behave in the wild before drawing conclusions from life in a tank."],
 ["F","Perhaps the greatest puzzle is why such abilities evolved at all. Most animals regarded as intelligent, such as apes, dolphins and crows, live for many years in social groups and learn a great deal from others. Octopuses, by contrast, are largely solitary, and many species live for only one or two years. The young usually never meet their parents, so each generation must discover the world for itself. The evolutionary biologist Sofia Lindqvist believes this shows that intelligence arose in octopuses entirely separately from its development in vertebrates, driven by the demands of hunting and avoiding predators rather than by social life."],
 ["G","For the time being, the octopus remains something of a scientific mystery. What seems increasingly clear is that intelligence need not take a single form, and that a creature whose last common ancestor with humans lived more than half a billion years ago can arrive at some strikingly similar solutions by a very different route. Studying it may therefore help scientists understand not only octopuses but also the range of ways in which minds can be built."]
 ],
